I'll review the semver port read-only against the pinned upstream oracle, skipping closed round-2 items and recorded deviations. First I'll load the review surfaces and the binding decisions.Round 2 already recorded the wildcard and intersection defects. I'll compare the current semver tree with the pinned oracle and the binding decisions, and only raise what those sources still miss.Desugar and schema metadata from round 2 look fixed. Next I’ll diff membership, formatting, and algebra against the pinned oracle and drop the deviations already on the ledger.I'll pin the desugar and grammar lines against node-semver and the ledger before writing any finding.### fable-3-1
- file: scratchpad/effected/semver/internal/desugar.ts:171
- class: bug   severity: required
- standard: D9, D11, section 14 (`upstream-bug`); README.md:68 node-semver range dialect. evidence: `parsePartial` (grammar.ts:302) keeps parsing `.minor` and `.patch` after a null component. `desugarXRange` then treats `major === null` as a full wildcard (desugar.ts:171–174) and `minor === null` as `M.x` (desugar.ts:177–180), dropping a concrete later component. The same branches are in the pinned oracle `desugar.ts:117` and `:122`. node-semver `invalidXRangeOrder` (`node_modules/semver/classes/range.js:280–283`) returns that comparator unchanged (`range.js:414–416`); `Comparator.parse` then throws `Invalid comparator` (`comparator.js:36–42`). `*.1` and `>=*.1` therefore become `>=0.0.0` here (so `5.0.0` matches) and `>*.1` becomes `<0.0.0-0` (no version matches). `1.*.2` and `1.x.2` become `>=1.0.0 <2.0.0-0`, so `1.9.0` matches. The property spelling drops a patch whenever `minor === null` (`desugar.property.test.ts:20`), and the coverage cases are well-ordered, so the green suite never parses these inputs. `~1.*.2` is a different path and already ignores the patch.
- failure: `Range.parse` accepts out-of-order X-ranges node-semver rejects, and either matches versions outside any reading of the trailing component or matches nothing.
- fix: On the plain/operator path in `parseSimple` (grammar.ts:401), fail the parse when `major === null && (minor !== null || patch !== null)` or `minor === null && patch !== null`, before `desugarXRange`. Add `Range.parse` regressions for `*.1`, `*.1.2`, `x.1`, `1.*.2`, `1.x.2`, `>=*.1`, and `>*.1`. Leave tilde and caret on their own expanders. The orchestrator records the upstream-bug deviation.

### fable-3-2
- file: scratchpad/effected/semver/internal/desugar.ts:246
- class: bug   severity: required
- standard: D9, D11, section 14 (`upstream-bug`); node-semver `hyphenReplace`. evidence: `lowerVersion` is `sv(lower.major ?? 0, lower.minor ?? 0, lower.patch ?? 0, lower.prerelease)` (lab desugar.ts:246; pinned oracle desugar.ts:170). A concrete component after a wildcard survives. node-semver `hyphenReplace` (`range.js:514–524`) sets the lower bound to empty when the major is an X, and to `>=M.0.0` when the minor is an X, ignoring a later patch. So `x.2 - 1.0.0` and `*.2 - 1.0.0` are `>=0.2.0 <=1.0.0` here and `<=1.0.0` in node-semver (`0.1.0` matches only there). `1.*.2 - 2.0.0` and `1.x.2 - 2.0.0` are `>=1.0.2 <=2.0.0` here and `>=1.0.0 <=2.0.0` in node-semver (`1.0.0` matches only there). `x.2.3 - 2.0.0` similarly floors at `0.2.3`. No hyphen fixture uses a wildcard lower bound with a later concrete component. A fully wildcard lower such as `* - 1.0.0` already has the same membership as `<=1.0.0` for every valid version.
- failure: Hyphen ranges whose lower bound has a wildcard before a concrete minor or patch reject versions node-semver accepts.
- fix: In `desugarHyphen`, emit no lower bound when `lower.major === null`, and use `>=M.0.0` with an empty prerelease when `lower.minor === null`. Keep the partial-upper expansion. Add `Range.parse` and membership regressions for `x.2 - 1.0.0`, `x.2.3 - 2.0.0`, and `1.x.2 - 2.0.0`, including `0.1.0` and `1.0.0`. The orchestrator records the upstream-bug deviation.

### fable-3-3
- file: scratchpad/effected/semver/internal/desugar.ts:98
- class: bug   severity: required
- standard: D9, D11, section 14 (`upstream-bug`); node-semver `replaceCaret`. evidence: After the null-major early return, `lower` is `sv(major, minor ?? 0, patch ?? 0, p.prerelease)` (lab desugar.ts:98; pinned oracle desugar.ts:62). A null minor still keeps a concrete patch. node-semver `replaceCaret` (`range.js:356–359`) returns `>=M.0.0 <(M+1).0.0-0` as soon as the minor is an X, and the `M === "0"` arm returns `>=0.0.0 <1.0.0-0`, both ignoring patch and prerelease. `^1.*.2` and `^1.x.2` are therefore `>=1.0.2 <2.0.0-0` here and `>=1.0.0 <2.0.0-0` in node-semver (`1.0.0` and `1.0.1` match only there). `^0.*.2` is `>=0.0.2 <1.0.0-0` here and `>=0.0.0 <1.0.0-0` in node-semver. A prerelease on that concrete patch, which `parsePartial` attaches only when `patch !== null` (grammar.ts:311), also grants a tuple permission node-semver never creates. `desugarTilde` already returns on `minor === null` without reading `patch` (desugar.ts:61). The caret coverage list uses ordered partials only.
- failure: Caret ranges with a wildcard minor and a concrete patch exclude versions node-semver includes in the lower bound, and can admit a prerelease tuple the caret expansion does not have.
- fix: When `minor === null`, build the caret lower bound as `major.0.0` with an empty prerelease, then keep the existing upper-bound arms. Add caret parse and membership regressions for `^1.*.2`, `^1.x.2`, and `^0.*.2`, including `1.0.0` and `0.0.0`. The orchestrator records the upstream-bug deviation.

